--
N5008 [temp.inst]/4, [temp.point]/1: a cvise-reduced, header-free model of the
libstdc++ std::get<0> chain (std::get -> std::__get_helper ->
_Tuple_impl::_M_head).  Well-defined and returns 42 under a conforming compiler
(verified with g++).

KNOWNBUG: the called __get_helper<0> specialization is deferred to the
method-body drain, but its body conversion throws and the body is dropped,
leaving a bodyless function that returns a nondet reference ("no body for
callee __get_helper<0>").

Root cause (traced): __get_helper has a non-deduced trailing parameter pack
_Tail that appears only in the body, so for __get_helper<0> it should be empty.
But the call is resolved from within get<0>'s body, and deduction gives
__get_helper<0> a spurious extra template argument bleeding from get<0>'s
element pack, so _Tail is recorded with size 1 instead of 0.  Resolving the
body qualifier _Tuple_impl<__i, int, _Tail...> then expands a phantom
one-element _Tail... with no bound element type and throws; the deferred drain
silently nil's the body.  (The "struct member must not be of code type" symex
invariant seen when the body is not nil'd is a downstream symptom.)  Flip to
CORE once __get_helper<0>'s _Tail is correctly empty: assertion 1 must SUCCEED
and assertion 2 (a wrong value) must FAIL, proving non-vacuity.
